To get your router working correctly, secure, and optimized for speed, you need to configure a few essential settings. [1] (https://www.hp.com/us-en/tech-takes/connectivity/how-to/how-to-set-up-a-router.html), [2] (https://www.microcenter.com/site/mc-news/article/complete-guide-to-router-settings.aspx)
First, ensure your hardware is properly connected: plug an Ethernet cable from your internet modem's output into the WAN (or Internet) port on your router. Then, log into the administration panel by typing your router's local IP address (typically 192.168.1.1 or 192.168.0.1) into a web browser. [1] (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=QcS0IP-nI3Y&t=246), [2] (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HTYR1BsogwM&t=27), [3] (https://www.hp.com/us-en/tech-takes/connectivity/how-to/how-to-set-up-a-router.html)
Once you are logged in, apply the following correct configuration settings:

1. Internet Connection Type

* Setting: Automatic / Dynamic IP (DHCP)
* Why: The vast majority of home Internet Service Providers (ISPs) automatically assign your connection details. (Note: If your ISP specifically gave you a username and password for the connection, select PPPoE instead). [1] (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iW-88XOKVCM&t=26), [2] (https://www.hp.com/us-en/tech-takes/connectivity/how-to/how-to-set-up-a-router.html)

2. Wi-Fi & Security Settings

* Network Name (SSID): Set a unique name. Do not hide your SSID, as hiding it actually creates connection issues for modern devices.
* Security Protocol: Set to WPA3 Personal or WPA2/WPA3 Transitional. Avoid old standards like WEP or TKIP, which are insecure and slow down your speed.
* Wi-Fi Password: Create a strong, unique password. [1] (https://support.apple.com/en-us/102766), [2] (https://www.popsci.com/router-security-settings/), [3] (https://www.youtube.com/shorts/rTJW5roSqhc), [4] (https://www.hp.com/us-en/tech-takes/connectivity/how-to/how-to-set-up-a-router.html)

3. Critical Security Toggles (Turn Off These Threats)

* Router Admin Password: Change the default factory login password (e.g., "admin") immediately to a personalized one. [1] (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=QxFkpgXryKQ), [2] (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HTYR1BsogwM&t=27)
* WPS (Wi-Fi Protected Setup): Disable. This feature allows hackers to easily brute-force their way into your Wi-Fi. [1] (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vzod-0wHcpk&vl=en), [2] (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HTYR1BsogwM&t=27)
* UPnP (Universal Plug and Play): Disable. While convenient for automatic device port-opening, it leaves a massive security hole for malware. [1] (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HTYR1BsogwM&t=27), [2] (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=QxFkpgXryKQ)
* Remote Management / Remote Administration: Disable. This ensures nobody can try to log into your router's settings from outside your physical home network. [1] (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mJnIgjyjEtc), [2] (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iW-88XOKVCM&t=26)

4. Performance Optimization

* 2.4 GHz Band Channels: Set the channel width to 20 MHz, and manually choose channel 1, 6, or 11 to avoid overlapping wireless interference from neighbors.
* 5 GHz Band Channels: Set the channel width to Auto (or 80 MHz if you want maximum speed close to the router).
* DNS Server (Optional but Recommended): Change from "Automatic" to "Manual" and use a fast, secure provider. Try Cloudflare DNS (Primary: 1.1.1.1, Secondary: 1.0.0.1) or Google DNS (Primary: 8.8.8.8, Secondary: 8.8.4.4) to speed up how fast web pages load. [1] (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4WxD6ixAvhc&t=5), [2] (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=QxFkpgXryKQ), [3] (https://support.apple.com/en-us/102766), [4] (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HTYR1BsogwM&t=27)
